"kalach" <scorduan@gmail.com> wrote:
>As with another poster,  I often find that I am driven deeper in the
>dungeon than I'd like to go (and my AC would allow), just to keep from
>getting hungry. This leads me to bury about 95% of my characters at or
>before minetown.
>
>Is there any good strategy to deal with this?

Yes.

Don't be afraid to pray to deal with being Weak with hunger.

Don't hang about searching endlessly "just in case"; only look for secret
doors when you actually need to.  Find traps by indirect methods whenever
you can.  In the early reaches of the main dungeon, traps are not much
of a threat.

How much time do you spend resting?  You may be being overly cautious
about being at full HP and/or Pw.

>Can you stand in one
>place and wait for monsters to come to you without getting hungry as
>quickly?

No.  Not only that, but doing so is a very bad strategy; what you need
is equipment, and most early monsters provide little or no equipment to
their slayers.  Killing monsters makes you higher level; being higher
level makes the game throw nastier monsters at you.  If you haven't got
the resources to deal with those nastier monsters, you will die.

When playing valkyrie/barbarian/samurai/knight, head for the Gnomish
Mines and kill dwarves (consider doing this even if you're Lawful; the
alignment penalty for killing monsters who started life peaceful is
pitifully small compared to the alignment bonuses you'll earn from all
the non-peaceful opponents you meet).

With flimsier characters (e.g. Wizard), a crash-dive (accompanied by
your pet) past the Oracle to the entrance to Sokoban may well be justified;
Sokoban has a great deal of food and magic, as well as the prize at the
end, and by getting there while still at a low level of experience, you
make the game generate weaker monsters there than would otherwise be the
case.
